e evidence.", "watch": "Whether the most recent high-quality polls showing Collins ahead are an outlier or a real shift toward the incumbent.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Susan Collins (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Maine's Senate race is one of the tightest in the country, with four-term Republican incumbent Susan Collins defending her seat against Democratic state official Troy Jackson. Recent polls are split: several show Jackson narrowly ahead, while a New York Times/Siena survey in late September gave Collins a three-point lead, her first of the cycle. Averages across outlets put Jackson up by roughly one to three points.\n\nMarkets price Jackson's chances in the low sixties, and expert ratings divide between Tossup and Lean D. Collins holds a substantial fundraising and cash-on-hand edge, but the national environment favors Democrats in a state that leans slightly their way. The race sits on a knife's edge heading into the final weeks.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7341, "margin": 3.16}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6906, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.626, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6915, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5751, "margin": 1.5}]}